st harness already exists that dlopens BOTH shared libraries and compares them.\n\n## Read first (mandatory)\n1. `tests/HARNESS.md` — how to write a test with this harness.\n2. `tests/common/mod.rs`, `tests/common/helpers.rs`, `tests/common/types.rs` — the harness.\n3. `tests/write_read.rs` and `tests/smoke.rs` — canonical examples. Match their style.\n4. `tests/common/api.rs` is GENERATED (do not edit) — grep it for the exact Rust signature of any libpng symbol, e.g. `grep -n 'png_muldiv' tests/common/api.rs`.\n\n## Your task\nCreate **`tests/lowlevel.rs`**: differential tests for the LOWEST-LEVEL exported\nentry points — the internal (`pngpriv.h`) functions that libpng exports but that\nare not part of `png.h`. These are listed as rows **C-1 … C-25** in `CONFIGS.md`\n(read that file's rows C-1..C-25; skip C-7, C-21, C-24 which other files cover).\n\nConcretely, write one `#[test]` per row, named exactly as the \"Row → test mapping\"\ntable in CONFIGS.md says (e.g. row C-3 maps to `lowlevel::muldiv`, so\n`#[test] fn muldiv()`). The rows to cover here:\n\n- `byte_accessors`  (C-1)  png_get_uint_32/16/int_32/uint_31, png_save_uint_32/16/int_32\n- `sig_cmp`         (C-2)  png_sig_cmp\n- `muldiv`          (C-3)  png_muldiv, png_muldiv_warn\n- `reciprocal`      (C-4)  png_reciprocal, png_reciprocal2\n- `fixed`           (C-5)  png_fixed, png_fixed_ITU\n- `gamma_scalar`    (C-6)  png_gamma_significant, png_gamma_8bit_correct, png_gamma_16bit_correct, png_gamma_correct\n- `xyz_xy`          (C-8)  png_XYZ_from_xy, png_xy_from_XYZ\n- `fp_parse`        (C-9)  png_check_fp_number, png_check_fp_string\n- `ascii_from`      (C-10) png_ascii_from_fp, png_ascii_from_fixed\n- `safecat_format`  (C-11) png_safecat, png_format_number\n- `crc`             (C-12) png_reset_crc, png_calculate_crc\n- `row_ops`         (C-13) png_do_bgr, png_do_invert, png_do_packswap, png_do_swap, png_do_strip_channel\n- `read_filter_row` (C-14) png_read_filter_row\n- `write_find_filter` (C-15) png_write_find_filter\n- `combine_row`     (C-16) png_combine_row\n- `interlace_row`   (C-17) png_do_read_interlace, png_do_write_interlace\n- `check_ihdr`      (C-18) png_check_IHDR\n- `check_keyword`   (C-19) png_check_keyword\n- `zstream_error`   (C-20) png_zstream_error, png_reset_zstream\n- `palette_indexes` (C-22) png_do_check_palette_indexes, png_get_palette_max\n- `time_conv`       (C-25) png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer, png_convert_from_time_t, png_convert_from_struct_tm, png_convert_to_rfc1123\n\n## Method\nFor EACH function, first read its actual C definition (`grep -n '^png_muldiv' c_src/src/*.c`\nthen read the function) so you know exactly what it reads and writes, what\n`png_ptr` state it depends on, and what it returns. Then call it on BOTH libraries\nwith **many randomised inputs** (`Rng::new(<fixed seed>)`, hundreds to thousands\nof iterations per function) plus the boundary values named in the CONFIGS row, and\ncompare every observable: return value, every out-parameter, every byte of every\nbuffer the function may touch, and every warning/error message.\n\nNotes / hints:\n- Several of these take a `png_structrp`. Use `new_read(api)` / `new_write(api)` to\n  get one and `guarded(api, png, &mut || {...})` around calls that can `png_error`\n  (e.g. `png_fixed` on an out-of-range double, `png_check_IHDR` with bad data).\n  Compare the `Guard` value AND the message.\n- `png_read_filter_row`, `png_combine_row`, `png_do_read_interlace`,\n  `png_do_write_interlace`, `png_write_find_filter` need specific `png_struct`\n  fields set up (row_buf, prev_row, rowbytes, transformations, pass, width, ...).\n  Those fields are NOT reachable from outside, so for those functions drive them\n  the way a real caller does — i.e. set up a real read/write operation with the\n  matching shape and let libpng call them — OR, where the function takes all its\n  state as arguments (`png_do_bgr`, `png_do_invert`, `png_do_packswap`,\n  `png_do_swap`, `png_do_strip_channel`, `png_read_filter_row` takes\n  `png_row_infop, png_bytep, png_const_bytep, int filter`), call them directly with\n  a `png_row_info` you fill in yourself. READ THE C SIGNATURE AND BODY to decide\n  which case each one is; if a function needs unreachable internal state, cover it\n  through the public path that reaches it (a full write with that filter mask, a\n  full interlaced read, etc.) and say so in a comment naming the row.\n- `png_calculate_crc`/`png_reset_crc` mutate `png_ptr->crc`; observe the result via\n  `png_reset_crc` + a sequence of `png_calculate_crc` calls followed by... note the\n  crc field is not directly readable, so drive it through a real read where the CRC\n  check outcome is observable (correct CRC vs `chunk_bad_crc`), and ALSO call the\n  functions directly and observe that neither library errors. Read the C first.\n- `png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer(char out[29], png_const_timep ptime)` returns int;\n  compare the return AND all 29 bytes of the buffer (initialise the buffer to a\n  known pattern first so you can see exactly what was written).\n- Be careful never to pass an out-of-bounds pointer: allocate generously.\n\n## Iterate to green\nRun `timeout 600 ./check.sh --test lowlevel 2>&1 | tail -40` and fix until all\ntests pass.\n\nIf C and Rust DIVERGE:\n1. First make sure it is not a bug in your test (uninitialised buffer, wrong\n   buffer size, dangling pointer, reading more bytes than the function writes).\n2. Then read the C source carefully and fix the RUST translation in `src/`.\n   Use small surgical `Edit` calls; do not rewrite whole files. Never touch `c_src/`.\n3. Note every divergence you fixed.\n\n## Report back\n- the exact list of `#[test]` functions you wrote and which CONFIGS row each covers\n- `wc -l tests/lowlevel.rs`\n- the final `cargo test` result line\n- EVERY divergence found, with the C source location, the wrong Rust code, and the fix","outputFile":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-CVzjKq/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-ho