{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_x2aymq36pwfa3qkxzqyu6nwhmqhfwg66tofxedrgfmexm2qxkauq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01AvBETWA82DPfTXAnmMsWNz","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write transforms differential tests","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":true,"prompt":"You are working in `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-CVzjKq/translated_rust` (cd there for everything).\n\nThis project is a C-to-Rust translation of libpng. `c_src/` holds the ORIGINAL C (ground truth, NEVER modify anything under `c_src/`). `src/` holds the Rust translation. A differential test harness already exists that dlopens BOTH shared libraries and compares them.\n\n## Read first (mandatory)\n1. `tests/HARNESS.md` — how to write a test with this harness.\n2. `tests/common/mod.rs`, `tests/common/helpers.rs`, `tests/common/types.rs` — the harness.\n3. `tests/write_read.rs` — the canonical example. Match its style.\n4. `tests/common/api.rs` is GENERATED (do not edit) — grep it for the exact Rust signature of any libpng symbol.\n5. `CONFIGS.md` — your rows are the `transforms::*` rows in the \"Row → test mapping\" table.\n\n## Your task\nCreate **`tests/transforms.rs`** with these `#[test]` functions (names must match\nthe CONFIGS.md mapping):\n\n- `single` — ONE test looping over the 27 read transforms listed in CONFIGS.md\n  rows (the `transforms::single` rows): png_set_palette_to_rgb,\n  png_set_expand_gray_1_2_4_to_8, png_set_tRNS_to_alpha, png_set_expand,\n  png_set_expand_16, png_set_strip_16, png_set_scale_16, png_set_strip_alpha,\n  png_set_swap_alpha, png_set_invert_alpha, png_set_filler, png_set_add_alpha,\n  png_set_bgr, png_set_swap, png_set_packing, png_set_packswap, png_set_shift,\n  png_set_invert_mono, png_set_gray_to_rgb, png_set_rgb_to_gray(_fixed),\n  png_set_quantize, png_set_background(_fixed), png_set_alpha_mode(_fixed),\n  png_set_gamma(_fixed), png_set_interlace_handling,\n  png_set_read_user_transform_fn + png_set_user_transform_info,\n  png_set_check_for_invalid_index.\n  For each: build a random source image with every shape the transform applies to\n  (use `VALID_SHAPES`), write it with the C library once to get a file, then read\n  it back with BOTH libraries with that transform enabled, and compare the decoded\n  rows byte-for-byte plus everything `png_get_*` reports after\n  `png_read_update_info` (`log_info` does most of it) and every warning. Use MANY\n  randomised inputs per (transform, shape) pair — random image contents, random\n  transform parameters (filler values, shifts, background colours, gammas,\n  coefficients, quantize palettes/histograms/maximum_colors) from a fixed-seed\n  `Rng`. Split into several `#[test]` fns if it gets long (`single`, `single_2`,\n  ...), but cover every transform.\n- `combinations` — randomised combinations of 2..6 read transforms applied\n  together, over every shape; several hundred random combinations with a fixed seed.\n- `gamma_tables` (row C-7) — png_build_gamma_table / png_destroy_gamma_table via\n  png_read_update_info: bit depth 8 and 16 × file gamma × screen gamma pairs\n  (including equal and unity), driven with png_set_gamma / png_set_alpha_mode and\n  a gAMA/sRGB chunk present or absent. Compare the decoded rows (that is what the\n  gamma tables affect) and all warnings.\n- `update_info` — png_read_update_info called once, twice (libpng warns) and not\n  at all before reading rows.\n- `write_side` — the 10 write-side transforms in CONFIGS.md\n  (`transforms::write_side` rows): png_set_bgr, png_set_swap, png_set_packing,\n  png_set_packswap, png_set_shift, png_set_invert_mono, png_set_invert_alpha,\n  png_set_swap_alpha, png_set_filler (strip filler on write),\n  png_set_write_user_transform_fn + png_set_user_transform_info. Compare the\n  produced file bytes.\n- `mng_intrapixel` — png_permit_mng_features × filter method 0/64 ×\n  RGB/RGB_ALPHA 8 and 16 bit, on read and write. Note filter method\n  `PNG_INTRAPIXEL_DIFFERENCING` (64) is only legal with\n  `PNG_FLAG_MNG_FILTER_64` permitted and no PNG signature — read\n  `png_check_IHDR` in `c_src/src/png.c` and `png_do_read_intrapixel` /\n  `png_do_write_intrapixel` to see exactly when it applies.\n\n## Method\nFIRST read the relevant C: `c_src/src/pngrtran.c` (`png_init_read_transformations`,\n`png_do_read_transformations`, and each `png_set_*`), `c_src/src/pngwtran.c`,\n`c_src/src/pngtrans.c`. That tells you which shapes each transform applies to,\nwhat it warns about, and what row size it produces. Remember `png_read_row` needs\n`png_get_rowbytes` AFTER `png_read_update_info` — the helper `read_image` already\ndoes that.\n\n`read_image(api, &file, &opts, &mut |api, png, info| { ...install transforms... })`\nis the easiest way in: the `setup` closure runs right after `png_read_info`.\n\nRecord aggressively. Also record the return value of transforms that have one\n(`png_set_interlace_handling`, `png_get_rgb_to_gray_status`).\n\n## Iterate to green\nRun `timeout 600 ./check.sh --test transforms 2>&1 | tail -40` and fix until green.\n\nIf C and Rust DIVERGE:\n1. First rule out a bug in your test (wrong row buffer size after\n   update_info, dangling pointer, comparing uninitialised padding).\n2. Then read the C source and fix the RUST translation in `src/` with small\n   surgical `Edit` calls. Never touch `c_src/`.\n3. Note every divergence you fixed.\n\n## Report back\n- the list of `#[test]` fns and the CONFIGS rows each covers\n- `wc -l tests/transforms.rs`\n- the final `cargo test` result line\n- EVERY divergence found, with C source location, wrong Rust code, and the fix"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2419,"cache_read_input_tokens":226875,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2419,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"309de29f-cb7a-4138-9016-7ab2e7ef6079","uuid":"47e4f6a5-197b-45d7-b0c8-e5d8e41fe13e","timestamp":"2026-08-23T02:18:19.926Z"}
