ces in the Rust translation** — `src/` is untouched, and `c_src/` was never modified. Both initial failures were bugs in my own test, confirmed against the C source:\n\n1. `single_6`, case `user d=8 ch=1 ct=0 bd=1`: C produced `[6d,00,00,00,0c,…]`, Rust `[6d,fe,fd,fc,00,…]`. Cause: `png_set_user_transform_info` with a user pixel depth *larger* than the row really has makes `png_combine_row` copy bytes past the real row data out of `png_ptr->big_row_buf`, which `png_read_start_row` (`c_src/src/pngrutil.c:4603-4614`) allocates with `png_malloc` — not `png_calloc` — for non-interlaced images. The differing bytes were uninitialised heap. Fixed by only varying the override *downwards* (`depth <= bit_depth`, `channels <= channels_of(ct)` in `single_6`; `{0,1}×{0,1}` in `combinations`), which keeps every compared byte defined.\n\n2. `single_5`, case `background+expand+gamma code=2 ct=3 bd=1`: palette entry 1 differed after `png_read_update_info`. Cause: my random `png_color_16` background had full 16-bit components, but the palette branch of `png_init_read_transformations` (`c_src/src/pngrtran.c:1712-1718`) indexes the 256-entry `gamma_table`/`gamma_to_1` with `background.red/green/blue` directly — an out-of-bounds read inside libpng. Every other colour type only feeds the background through `png_gamma_correct` (`c_src/src/png.c:3356`), which is purely computational and safe for any 16-bit value. Fixed with `rand_back()`, which clamps components to 0..255 for palette images only.\n\nA third, non-divergence fix: the reference writer refused the deliberately out-of-range palette indices needed for C-93, because `png_struct::num_palette_max` is `0` (check *enabled*) in a freshly calloc'ed struct and a write-side `png_benign_error` is fatal in this build (`c_src/src/pngwrite.c:398-406`). `make_file` now calls `png_set_check_for_invalid_index(png, 0)` before writing; this does not change the produced bytes.\n\nNote: `tests/common/{mod.rs,sweep.rs,types.rs}` and `tests/sweep.rs` were being edited concurrently by another agent while I worked; I did not touch them, and `sweep.rs` had transient compile errors early on that resolved themselves."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":427,"cache_read_input_tokens":338001,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":427,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01AvBETWA82DPfTXAnmMsWNz","session_id":"309de29f-cb7a-4138-9016-7ab2e7ef6079","uuid":"a57c5441-ea17-4c85-855a-1e22dcb67357","timestamp":"2026-08-23T02:47:48.605Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write transforms differential tests"}
