\n## Two things worth knowing\n\n1. **The task brief says `png_read_png` \"must reject\" the `PNG_TRANSFORM_STRIP_FILLER*` flags — it does not.** `png_read_png` has no code for them at all (`pngread.c:1029`, \"We don't handle adding filler bytes\"), so they are silently ignored. The test asserts that ignoring, in both libraries. (Symmetrically, the read-only flags are ignored by `png_write_png`.) Only `BEFORE|AFTER` together produces a diagnostic, and only on the *write* side.\n\n2. **`png_combine_row` (`c_src/src/pngrutil.c:3257-3276`, `3705`) preserves the padding bits of a row's last byte from the destination buffer** — including on the plain non-interlaced `memcpy` path. When `png_read_png` allocates the rows itself they come from `png_malloc` (not zeroed), so for sub-byte pixel depths with a partial last byte those bits are uninitialised heap. The test handles this by running two arrangements: `Rows::Own` (the test installs its own zeroed rows with `png_set_rows`; `info_ptr->free_me` has no `PNG_FREE_ROWS` so `png_read_png` leaves them alone — fully deterministic, every byte compared) and `Rows::Lib` (libpng allocates; the final byte is blanked when the row has padding bits). Widths 8/16/32 give zero padding for every sub-byte depth, so even `Rows::Lib` compares every byte there. `round_trip` uses `Rows::Own` only, otherwise those uninitialised bits would leak into the rewritten file.\n\nNote: mid-run I saw `src/gen/pngwrite_p04.rs:237` briefly read `16383` instead of the C's `16384` (`pngwrite.c:1679`); it reverted on its own — a concurrent agent's own mutation check, not a real bug."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":351,"cache_read_input_tokens":248710,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":351,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01UP6NJVScwKVWsWLKn6DXBk","session_id":"3